  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 6.3. Increased capacity of Member States to develop gender-responsive legislation, policies and measures for a world of work free from violence and harassment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In May 2023, the Government of Malawi adopted the Public Service Workplace Anti-Sexual Harassment Policy which defines and prohibits workplace sexual harassment and details procedures for handling complaints in the Public Service for a safe and harassment-free working environment. This is a measure that has been undertaken as a measure towards the ratification and implementation of ILO Convention No. 190 and Recommendation No. 206. This is in line with the key principles of the C190 instrument that respects, promotes and realizes the right of everyone to a world of work free from violence and harassment and ensure right to equality and non-discrimination, including for women workers, as well as for vulnerable groups or groups in situations of vulnerability disproportionately affected by violence and harassment in the world of work. The guidelines provide the procedure for managing sexual harassment in the workplace from the time they are reported through to hearing processes and the resolution of the case and further rehabilitation of sexual harassment survivors. Users of these Guidelines include but not limited to Anti-Sexual Harassment Desk Officers (ASHDO), Disciplinary Committee, Human Resources Officers, Gender officers, Heads of Departments, Controlling/Responsible Officers and others. The Guidelines also outline institutional reporting structures for Sexual Harassment as a monitoring mechanism of occurrences of sexual harassment in the Public Service Workplace. The key broad areas of the Guidelines include; Categorisation of Sexual Harassment Cases, Management of Sexual Harassment Cases, Rehabilitation and protection of Sexual Harassment Survivors and Sexual Harassment Safeguards. The guidelines are standardizing handling of sexual harassment grievances in the Public Service, providing clear redress mechanisms for sexual harassment cases and ensuring that all incidents of sexual harassment are handled with the seriousness they require. With support from ILO AIDS, the ILO provided convened a national meeting to discuss the development of the Malawi Public Service Workplace Anti-Sexual Harassment Policy which were adopted into the Policy during a validation meeting in June 2022. The provided inputs in the discussions. Further, in January 2023, during the validation meeting, the ILO provided comments and feedback in the draft guidelines and undertook a normative revision of the final guidelines. The approved guidelines by the Government incorporates the changes proposed by the ILO.</narrative>
   </description>
